WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.873
Stanley Cohen wanted to know whether a growth
factor could drive a chemical reaction in a
preparation with no living cells.

00:00:06.488 --> 00:00:12.593
He wrote that, as a biochemist, he expected a
hormone or growth factor to control some
chemical reaction.

00:00:13.409 --> 00:00:16.322
He thought this reaction could be shown in a
cell-free system.

00:00:16.802 --> 00:00:22.355
His model was Sutherland's work: adding glucagon
to cell membranes produced cyclic AMP.

00:00:22.993 --> 00:00:32.888
At Vanderbilt University at the end of the
1970s, Cohen's laboratory used membranes from
A-431 cells, a human tumour cell line with many
EGF receptors.

00:00:33.461 --> 00:00:35.388
There were no living cells in the preparation.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.440
Graham Carpenter did much of the biochemistry.

00:00:39.008 --> 00:00:44.866
When they added EGF (epidermal growth factor) to
the membranes, phosphate began to move onto
protein.

00:00:45.438 --> 00:00:47.013
Cohen wrote that he was exhilarated.

00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:50.631
Here was a cell-free system in which EGF had an
effect.

00:00:51.186 --> 00:00:55.099
Hirota Ushiro then identified the amino acid
that received the phosphate.

00:00:55.671 --> 00:00:56.646
It was tyrosine.

00:00:57.202 --> 00:01:00.944
At that time, tyrosine was not known as a site
for a regulatory phosphate.

00:01:01.503 --> 00:01:08.383
In a normal cell, phosphotyrosine is less than
1% of all the phosphate on proteins, and almost
no one had looked for it.

00:01:08.945 --> 00:01:12.166
The EGF receptor turned out to be the kinase
itself.
